The discussion focused on synthesizing findings from a report on the macroeconomic case for adaptation investment in light of climate change. Key messages included severe impending losses, where without stronger action, the average country may be significantly poorer by 2050, with some sub-Saharan African and Southeast Asian nations facing GDP losses over 20% from temperature shocks. It was noted that the least developed countries could already be 10% poorer today due to climate impacts. The conversation also highlighted that adaptation strategies represent a smart investment, delivering a median economic benefit-cost ratio of 4 to 1 and a median internal rate of return of 25%, positioning adaptation as one of the best economic investments available to mitigate future losses.
